{"id":"jerusalem","name":"Jerusalem","aliases":[],"sources":["nave","torrey","easton"],"topics":["nave:jerusalem","torrey:jerusalem"],"groups":["Called","Gates of","Buildings","Streets","Places in and around","Captured and pillaged by","Walls of, restored and fortified by","Besieged by","Roman rulers who resided in","Historical Notices Of"],"definition":{"source":"easton","term":"Jerusalem","text":"Called also Salem, Ariel, Jebus, the “city of God,” the “holy city;” by the modern Arabs el-Khuds, meaning “the holy;” once “the city of Judah” (2 Chr. 25:28). This name is in the original in the dual form, and means “possession of peace,” or “foundation of peace.” The dual form probably refers to the two mountains on which it was built, viz., Zion and Moriah; or, as some suppose, to the two parts of the city, the “upper” and the “lower city.” Jerusalem is a “mountain city enthroned on a mountain fastness” (comp. Ps. 68:15, 16; 87:1; 125:2; 76:1, 2; 122:3). It stands on the edge of one of the highest table-lands in Palestine, and is surrounded on the south-eastern, the southern, and the western sides by deep and precipitous ravines. It is first mentioned in Scripture under the name Salem (Gen. 14:18; comp. Ps. 76:2). When first mentioned under the name Jerusalem, Adonizedek was its king …"},"lemmas":[{"strong":"H8179","lemma":"שַׁעַר","xlit":"shaʻar","gloss":"an opening","kjv":"city, door, gate, port","count":45,"share":0.07},{"strong":"H2346","lemma":"חוֹמָה","xlit":"chôwmâh","gloss":"a wall of protection","kjv":"wall, walled","count":31,"share":0.05},{"strong":"H894","lemma":"בָּבֶל","xlit":"Bâbel","gloss":"Babel , including Babylonia and the Babylonian empire","kjv":"Babel, Babylon","count":34,"share":0.05},{"strong":"H6726","lemma":"צִיּוֹן","xlit":"Tsîyôwn","gloss":"Tsijon , a mountain of Jerusalem","kjv":"Zion","count":28,"share":0.04},{"strong":"H2396","lemma":"חִזְקִיָּה","xlit":"Chizqîyâh","gloss":"Chizkijah, a king of Judah, also the name of two other I","kjv":"